{#- Traffic Shift Away, as zebra-rs `set` statements.
    Port of dockers/docker-fpm-frr/frr/bgpd/tsa/bgpd.tsa.isolate.conf.j2.

    Rendered once per outbound policy in use (DeviceGlobalCfgMgr finds
    them in the running config). The entries go in below the policy's own
    `entry 100 action permit`, so while they are present:

      external policy:  20  the loopback, re-marked with the traffic-shift
                            community, is still advertised
                        30  so is a route carrying the internal tag
                        40  everything else is denied, i.e. withdrawn
      internal policy:  20  everything is advertised, with no-export added

    FRR's `set community C` takes the value inline; zebra-rs references a
    named community-set, so the two sets used are defined here. Without
    `additive` the set replaces the route's communities, as in FRR.

    Tried on a SONiC VS against an FRR peer: applying the external form
    withdraws a non-loopback network and re-advertises the loopback with
    the community; deleting the three entries restores both. -#}
{% if internal_route_map == '1' %}
set community-set TSA_NO_EXPORT members no-export
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 20 action permit
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 20 set community name TSA_NO_EXPORT
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 20 set community additive
{% else %}
set community-set TSA_COMMUNITY members {{ constants.bgp.traffic_shift_community }}
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 20 action permit
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 20 match prefix-set PL_Loopback{{ ip_version }}
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 20 set community name TSA_COMMUNITY
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 30 action permit
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 30 match tag {{ constants.bgp.internal_community_match_tag }}
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 30 set community name TSA_COMMUNITY
set policy {{ route_map_name }} entry 40 action deny
{% endif %}
